{{smaller block|And also ''Tourneys'' and exercyse of Armys fyrst founde [in Candia] on horsebake.
{{right|''Torkington'', Diarie of Eng. Travell, p. 19.}}
In these jousts and ''tourneys'', described with sufficient prolixity but in a truly heart-stirring tone by the chroniclers of the day, we may discern the last gleams of the light of chivalry. {{right|''Prescott'', Ferd. and Isa., ii. 11.}}}}

'''tourney-helm''' (tör′ni-helm), ''n.'' A helmet used in the tourneys of the fifteenth and sixteenth centuries, and peculiar in having the face-opening very large, and guarded only by light iron bars with wide spaces between them. In this respect it is the reverse of the tilting-helmet.

'''tourning†, tourneynge†''', ''a.'' Middle English forms of ''turning''.

'''tourniquet''' (tör′ni-ket), ''n.'' [Also ''torniquet''; < F. ''tourniquet'', a turnstile, sash-pulley, tourniquet in surgery, < ''tourner'', turn: see ''turn''.] 1†. A turnstile.

{{smaller block|Seek some winding alley with a ''tourniquet'' at the end of it, where chariot never rolled.
{{right|''Sterne'', Sentimental Journey, p. 49.}}}}

2. An instrument for arresting the passage of blood through an artery by means of compression effected with a screw. {{smaller|It is used to control hemorrhage temporarily, as in surgical operations on a limb, or to check the force of the blood-current in cases of aneurismal or other vascular tumors.}}—'''Hydraulic tourniquet.''' {{smaller|Same as ''Barker's mill'' (which see, under ''mill''<sup>1</sup>).}}

